1
00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:03.436
In this lesson, you'll build

2
00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:06.773
an evaluation framework
to systematically measure performance.

3
00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.376
All right. Let's go.

4
00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:14.080
So first, what exactly is the goal
of evaluating the model?

5
00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:18.618
Well, first it's to understand is your LLM
really improving or not and be able

6
00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:23.423
to have a quantitative response to that
that says yes or no that you can rely on.

7
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.326
The second goal is
where is it hallucinating?

8
00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:28.561
Where is it still being incorrect?

9
00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.430
Where you can still improve it? Right?

10
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.900
So what is the next
frontier of what it can improve on?

11
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:38.304
So evaluation is both
about asking the question of did it work?

12
00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:39.739
Did what I tried work?

13
00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:41.975
And also where can I take it?

14
00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.978
Where can I take the model
as I improve it?

15
00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:48.481
A good evaluation is one
that is quantitative.

16
00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.551
So one that you can actually have
measurable scores

17
00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:56.056
that you can actually,
you know, compare A versus B, model

18
00:00:56.056 --> 00:01:00.126
A versus model B, say one is good,
one is better and one is best.

19
00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.796
It can also point out
what you can improve next.

20
00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:07.600
So a good evaluation is not so far out
there in the either that it can't

21
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.438
actually tell you what you can
actually improve in this next iteration.

22
00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:15.909
A good evaluation is really setting up
this next frontier correctly.

23
00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:20.213
And the last thing is around
creating an evaluation that is scalable

24
00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:23.416
and automated,
and something that is magical about this

25
00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:27.187
new era is that you can use LLMs
to help you with this, so that you can

26
00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:31.191
programmatically start to evaluate
these models and be able to scale this out

27
00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:35.261
to larger and larger evaluation datasets,
as well as to more and more models.

28
00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:39.399
So what should your initial evaluation
dataset look like?

29
00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:43.536
I think I see a lot of people embark
on these epic journeys to create

30
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:48.508
evaluation datasets, but in fact,
I always encourage people to start small.

31
00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:51.311
Start with 20 examples, 20 to 100.

32
00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:52.612
And keeping the small,

33
00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:56.316
the whole goal is to move
very quickly, iterate very quickly,

34
00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:59.786
and improve one thing
so that you see improvement in the model.

35
00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:04.224
And then as you see improvement in the
model, your evaluation set will adjust.

36
00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:08.628
So actually committing to something too
large is not is not actually practical.

37
00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.830
Quality matters more than quantity.

38
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.534
So having high quality
evaluation is more important

39
00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:17.637
because then you can actually rely
on your evaluations not noisy.

40
00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:22.108
And then being able to do that again iterative expansion is extremely important.

41
00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:25.245
And finally focus on areas of improvement

42
00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:28.915
such as, you know, those hallucinations,
where is the model

43
00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:30.750
almost get enough, but not quite?

44
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.319
Where is that failing
that you want it to improve on?

45
00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:36.656
Again, this is setting
some Northstar, goals for the model

46
00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.358
for its next iteration.

47
00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:43.358
Here's an example of, an evaluation data
set, of ours, for the MBA dataset.

48
00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:46.766
As you can see, there's
actually quite a good set of breadth

49
00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:48.434
in the questions being asked.

50
00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.104
You know, who is a point guard
for the Golden State Warriors?

51
00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:52.272
What is the number of players

52
00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:55.141
on the Chicago Bulls
who are 25 years old or younger?

53
00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:58.878
One good way to think about this is
get enough breadth where you think,

54
00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:03.816
the real coverage of someone using
your LLM would actually need and require,

55
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.687
because this is teaching your LLM
the entire breadth of what it might see.

56
00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:10.957
So what are some practical tips?

57
00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:12.659
So that's that's all fine and good.

58
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.928
You set up your evaluation dataset.

59
00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:17.964
How do you actually really set up
that next frontier that I keep mentioning?

60
00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:22.969
Well, one good tip is to create examples
or find examples

61
00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:26.839
that are the easiest ones for the LLM,
but that they still fail.

62
00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.108
You know, it's barely almost there.

63
00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.276
You want it to get there.

64
00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:34.781
And so that's a really reasonable
next frontier for the LLM to conquer.

65
00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:38.585
The next approach is what I like
to call an adversarial playground.

66
00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:42.589
So hook your LLM up to a playground
and try to break it.

67
00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:43.223
Right.

68
00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:47.093
Like try to ask it questions,
get it to break in your setting

69
00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:50.230
So that you can understand
what the boundaries are.

70
00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:51.598
And oftentimes,

71
00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:55.535
this is how you might expect your users
to be using your model as well.

72
00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:57.537
Or someone else evaluate it.

73
00:03:57.537 --> 00:04:00.573
it's a good idea to actually put it
through the adversarial playground.

74
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:04.777
And finally, set
a next accuracy target for your LLM.

75
00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:05.945
What do I mean by this?

76
00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:09.315
Well, one kind of good
rule of thumb is think through

77
00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.218
how many nines essentially
you want in your LLM.

78
00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.320
Right.
Like how accurate do you really need it.

79
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.125
And then start with a really, really down
scoped, very focused evaluation data

80
00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:24.125
set and go from maybe the base accuracy
is like 20% go to 20, 30, 40, 50, 60.

81
00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:30.265
Get to that 90, 95% accuracy,
99% accuracy, whatever that target is.

82
00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:35.208
Get there first, so you believe it
can happen on a small evaluation set.

83
00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:38.011
And then make your evaluation
harder after that.

84
00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:40.713
And so then the accuracy drops
back down to 20.

85
00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:44.217
And then you move your way
back up to 90, 95, 99.

86
00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:47.220
Whatever your actual accuracy target is.

87
00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:49.756
So how do you actually score your output?

88
00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:50.923
I mentioned LLMs.

89
00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:55.561
So you can get the LLM to output
a score, of what

90
00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:56.963
it generated previously.

91
00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.232
This is a different LLM call, of course.

92
00:04:59.232 --> 00:05:02.769
and what you provided is the question,
the generated answer.

93
00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.938
In this case, whatever
the input output is, in the scoring

94
00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:09.642
method through the prompt of your LLM.
And one way

95
00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:14.113
to return, a reliable numerical score
is to use structured output.

96
00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:17.183
So you can specify that the score
should be an end to float

97
00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:21.054
a list of ints that need to match, maybe
like a product ID or something like that.

98
00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:24.857
So a way to score the model, in a way
that's quantitative

99
00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:26.793
and guaranteed to be quantitative.

100
00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:30.596
You can also, of course,
calculate, traditional exact matches

101
00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:34.334
between the output and the reference
output, of your evaluation.

102
00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:37.337
So as an example, you can see here,

103
00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.473
the system prompt says compare
the following two data frames.

104
00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.475
If they're similar, they're similar.

105
00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:43.609
If they're almost identical,

106
00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:47.847
are the conveyed the same information
about the NBA roster dataset and respond

107
00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:52.151
with a valid Json explanation string
and then similar bool.

108
00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:55.021
So responding with that bool
kind of like a score.

109
00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:56.089
Zero/one.

110
00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.958
And then you give it to the both
a reference

111
00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.994
data frame
as well as the generated data frame.

112
00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:02.829
And you can see here

113
00:06:02.829 --> 00:06:06.666
those are the outputted ones, down here
with Steph Curry and salary.

114
00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.903
And then you ask the LLM
can you tell me if these data frames

115
00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:13.940
are similar? And I can say, yes,
these are similar.

116
00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.442
So that's an example of SQL.

117
00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.644
Let's actually go one level deeper SQL.

118
00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:19.712
So that you understand for

119
00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:22.782
for this particular use case,
you can actually get pretty deep

120
00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:23.850
with your application.

121
00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.386
So eval is pretty application specific.

122
00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:29.989
So score similarity
of, of that generated to reference SQL.

123
00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:31.724
That's exactly what you just saw.

124
00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:36.095
The generated SQL, can be a semantic
match, but not always an exact match.

125
00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:37.597
And just to bring that example

126
00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:41.434
back again, you can see here
that the salary and name are inverted.

127
00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:42.735
So if you were to do

128
00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:46.706
an exact match on these two data frames,
it would have said false, when in reality

129
00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:50.076
this is totally fine
for the output of the SQL.

130
00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:51.177
It's correct.

131
00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:54.680
So you can actually be able to do
essentially fuzzy matches with LLMs.

132
00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.582
Now, it's

133
00:06:56.582 --> 00:07:01.354
also a pretty valuable to leverage
deterministic methods for evaluation.

134
00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.689
What I mean by
that is not using an LLM.

135
00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:07.126
So you can execute the generated SQL
against a database.

136
00:07:07.193 --> 00:07:10.997
And then you can compare exact match,
but executing against database will help

137
00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:14.967
you understand
whether, a query is even valid or not.

138
00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:17.470
So if it's invalid, it's
obviously incorrect.

139
00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:21.441
And then one bonus point with memory
tuning that you'll explore soon

140
00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:26.145
is that you can actually get away
with an exact match, evaluation criteria,

141
00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:30.683
if you teach the model to output,
a certain format or output, certain

142
00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:33.786
types of exact matches to make it easier
for you to do evaluation.

143
00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:37.690
So note that as you explore, evaluation
and you iterate

144
00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:42.195
on, on the model, you can actually update
what your evaluation criteria are.

145
00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:45.331
These iteration cycles
go go very much hand in hand.

146
00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:48.367
Speaking of iteration, as you improve it,

147
00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:51.070
you'll continue to expand that evaluation
dataset.

148
00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.104
It'll have more breadth.

149
00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:55.808
It will cover more things like maybe you
play with in the adversarial playground.

150
00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:59.212
It'll be able to cover harder examples.

151
00:07:59.212 --> 00:08:02.682
You will also want to improve
your scoring mechanisms.

152
00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:04.083
Start with something easy.

153
00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:07.253
but improve
how you're actually evaluating the model.

154
00:08:07.253 --> 00:08:11.057
Whether it's those deterministic systems,
like being able to do exact match

155
00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:14.460
or checking
if it's valid SQL or it's using LLMs

156
00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.964
and different prompts
to improve what the output score that bool is.

157
00:08:17.964 --> 00:08:22.235
Maybe having a bool isn't the way to go,
maybe it's actually evaluating the SQL

158
00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:26.138
query across many different dimensions,
not just whether the output is

159
00:08:26.138 --> 00:08:26.939
similar or not.

160
00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:30.209
Maybe you care about the efficiency of
of the SQL query coming out,

161
00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:33.713
Adding harder, harder examples
as you iterate.

162
00:08:33.713 --> 00:08:37.683
Again, setting that next frontier
incredibly, incredibly important.

163
00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:40.019
And then being rigorous.

164
00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:43.956
So this is more of a process tip,
be rigorous about tracking

165
00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:47.760
which models actually produce
which results because you're going to

166
00:08:47.927 --> 00:08:49.362
create a lot of different iterations.

167
00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:51.130
Sometimes they're going to be
small iterations.

168
00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.799
And naming these things can be tough.

169
00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:56.969
you know, in general with
computer science and software engineering,

170
00:08:56.969 --> 00:08:57.637
it can be tough.

171
00:08:57.637 --> 00:09:00.640
But here it's it's incredibly important
to be able to keep track.

